/! What I Learned					
section
	.container
		.row
			.col-lg-12
				h2 What I learned
				p.lead.mb-3 This project reinforced the importance of designing around both audience and information needs, especially when a website serves several groups with different goals. Organizing a large amount of program information challenged me to think carefully about hierarchy, accessibility, and creating clear pathways without overwhelming users.
				p.lead.mb-3 It also gave me the opportunity to expand an existing identity into a more complete digital visual system. Working from the client’s logo, I explored typography, bespoke graphic elements, layouts, and interface styles that could work cohesively across the website while still feeling connected to the original brand.
				p.lead.mb-3 Carrying that direction from Figma into a custom Gutenberg theme strengthened the connection between my design and development process. Building reusable custom blocks required me to think beyond individual page designs and consider how the visual system could translate into flexible components that remained consistent as content changed.
				.d-inline.mb-5.mb-md-0
					= link_to 'View Website <i class="fad fa-external-link-alt"></i>'.html_safe, 'https://careerbound.baltimorespromise.org/', target: "_blank"
				.squiggle-vertical					
						

